<Base title="Teaching"><section class="wrap pagehero"><div class="eyebrow">Teaching & education</div><h1>Teaching what matters, and why.</h1><p>I'm interested in teaching computer science and software development at the college level. My approach starts with fundamentals, clear explanations, and the habits that make someone effective on a real software project.</p></section><section class="wrap section" style="padding-top:0"><div class="bodycopy"><h2>What I bring to the classroom</h2><p>I've spent more than 15 years working on production software: designing applications, writing code, integrating systems, maintaining existing projects, and helping clients understand and use the tools built for them.</p><p>That work has included creating user documentation and providing hands-on training. Teaching programming is different from training software users, but both require patience, clarity, and an ability to explain unfamiliar ideas in practical terms.</p><h2>How I think about learning software</h2><p>Students need to understand the fundamentals: how programs execute, how data moves through a system, how to reason about errors, and how to test whether something works. Practical projects help connect those ideas to a larger purpose.</p><p>AI tools are changing how software gets written. I believe students should learn to use them thoughtfully without outsourcing their understanding or responsibility for the result.</p><h2>Education</h2><div class="listcards"><div class="listcard"><div class="date">Completed · August 2026</div><h3>BS, Software Engineering</h3><p>Western Governors University</p></div><div class="listcard"><div class="date">In progress</div><h3>MS, Software Engineering — DevOps</h3><p>Western Governors University · 13 graduate competency units completed through the accelerated BS-to-MS pathway as of the September 2026 transcript</p></div></div><h2>Instructional materials</h2><p>I'm developing sample lessons and exercises for introductory programming and software engineering. They'll be published here as they are ready.</p><div class="note"><strong>Interested in discussing a course?</strong><p style="margin-bottom:0">I'd welcome a conversation about computer science, programming, or software development instruction. <a class="textlink" href="mailto:colejd@gmail.com">Email me</a>.</p></div></div></section></Base>
